{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Arise, shine.</b> Imperative mood, like <small>come (II Samuel 13:11)</small>; it comes with holem because of the alef." "<b>For your light has come.</b> As praise. In my opinion, the coming of the light is darkness, as when dusk comes and the light you had is cut off — <b>and the glory of YHWH shines upon you.</b> The proof: \"the sun shall no more be your light by day\" [below, v. 19]. The meaning of \"shine\" concerns the kingdom or prophecy."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 60, :notes {1 "The lemma reads אורך (your light) but the comment seems to address the phrase כי בא אורך as a whole; the interpretation that 'the coming of the light is darkness' is rendered hyper-literally as the Hebrew demands."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:00:24.798515524Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}